@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+# ECON-02B2d18b — Authoritative site-local generic factory integration
+
+**Date:** 2026-11-01
+**Baseline:** `SingleGalaxy_ECON02B2d18a_Generic_Factory_Execution_Source_Tests_2026-11-01.zip` (verified)
+**Scope:** Integrate the reusable d18a execution engine into one real colony writer; approve **only** the fixed aluminum processor/finisher/conveyor template until arbitrary recipes, NPC transport, and placement authority are implemented.
+
+## Implementation
+
+1. The main `Simulation` now owns a `site_id -> FactoryExecution` map. Its step loop advances installed factories alongside other colony facilities; power/staffing/stock checks in the deterministic generic execution engine still apply. This is **not** a second database writer.
+2. Factory creation/installation/load/unload/operator/shutdown use six dedicated `Action` variants. The command's issuer, ship, site, tick, ID, parameters and outcome are journaled through `PersistentWorld`.
+3. Only owned and built sites are eligible. The first immutable configuration is an aluminum processor (#100), finisher (#200), and three-cell conveyor (#800). Machine/conveyor construction debits **real** staging-depot modules; aluminum/panel/frame/scrap transfers debit or credit **real** site warehouse goods in kilograms. Fabrication remains mass-balanced and capacity-bounded.
+4. Save format **v41** persists canonical, length-delimited hexadecimal configuration plus mutable checkpoint and operator assignments; older v39/v40 saves continue to load, as verified by the legacy regression group. Reconstructed checkpoints reject malformed configuration/state. The authoritative total-material check includes installed modules and the factory's internal inventory.
+5. A `generic_factory_transfer` SQLite receipt binds completed installation and inventory transfers to command IDs, ticks, quantities, and exact source/destination account names. Replay rejects missing, duplicated or forged transfer provenance; the negative test mutates an actual receipt in a copied database and confirms restore refusal.
+6. The trusted C++ colony `Control` and `construction::Plans` allocate durable command IDs and validate requested site and actions. The existing localhost relay applies token, same-origin and schema restrictions, never deriving issuer/ship identity from browser JSON. Colony UI includes an **experimental generic factory** panel; its initial API is bounded to the approved IDs and one recipe chain.
+
+## Verified local tests
+
+- Full Linux CMake/Ninja build: **passed**.
+- Native CTest standard group: **78/78 passed**.
+- Live/relay group: **33/34 passed in the initial parallel run** before the run was interrupted by a stuck pre-existing retirement HTTP test; that last test **passed in an isolated rerun (1/1)**. Each of **112 registered CTest cases** therefore has a passing run, but **not** as a single uninterrupted 112-test invocation.
+- JavaScript `node --test tests/*.js`: **93/93 passed**. The two `.cjs` programs require explicit executable arguments and are not part of that 93-test command.
+- Three offline Chromium controls smoke tests **passed**: original structures, existing crews and new experimental generic-factory controls. These browser runs use a mocked HTTP transport; native HTTP security and persistence are separately verified by `ECON02B2d18b_GenericFactory_HTTP`.
+- New site integration C++ test: material-conservation across batches, stable v41 snapshots, SQLite recovery, owner authorization, worker assignment, resource depletion and tamper detection.
+- New HTTP integration Python test: session/site/origin/payload rejection, real module debit, no free material transfer, server restart preserving operator and shutdown flags.
+
+## Mandatory remaining gates — NOT CLAIMED COMPLETE
+
+- This is one **fixed, authorized aluminum template** only; arbitrary player-authored generic factory layouts/recipes and new machine types are not admitted to the live world.
+- Generic warehouse-to-machine transfer currently changes two **site-local authoritative accounts**, but is **not** an NPC-carried spatial delivery; path/hauling, source selection and backpressure integration need a subsequent gate. Do not represent it as a physically simulated journey.
+- Operator identity is a real resident and cannot be doubly assigned in generic machines or the existing two-stage workshop; however, the other NPC construction-job scheduler is not yet fully coordinated. Automatic travel, work shifts, worker fatigue, full manufacturing job priorities, and generic NPC dismantling are not implemented.
+- The approved template's fixed world-grid placement does **not** yet validate collisions with all other colony fixture/building footprints. Do not enable unrestricted layout creation before that validation.
+- Generic machines use the execution engine's existing `Machine` power field; there is no complete site utility-grid authorization/consumption binding yet.
+- Windows native compilation and GUI tests, 3D pathfinding, generic warehouse evacuation and unrestricted graph retirement remain open.
+
+## Next integration gate
+
+1. Validate actual world-grid machine, link and NPC access, rejecting conflicts with buildings/fixtures.
+2. Replace direct warehouse-machine stock movement with named-worker/real conveyor custody and capacity-limited deliveries.
+3. Add proper worker schedules, shared reservation checks, power/maintenance accounting, and generic NPC shutdown/retirement.
+4. Provide a player-approved versioned catalog for arbitrary valid machinery and recipes before allowing general-purpose factory execution.
